      "Distances are searoute 1.6.0 over Eurostat's SeaRoute network, the line the map draws. Each route has a routing for each leg (the shortest with the canals open, or Suez, Panama or both avoided) and the table lists every routing that changes the distance. The laden leg runs from the load port to the discharge port; the ballast leg back from the discharge port, the Baltic's TCE delivery point.",
      "Miles inside emission control areas are measured on the same line against polygons built from MARPOL Annex VI Appendix VII and the area regulations, with the Mediterranean ECA from 1 May 2025 and the Canadian Arctic and Norwegian Sea ECAs from 1 March 2027. Inside them a ship without a scrubber burns MGO at 0.10 percent sulphur; outside, VLSFO at 0.50 percent.",
      "Bunkers are priced at the hub nearest the load port among the four with public prices for every grade (Singapore, Fujairah, Rotterdam, Houston), the Baltic's rule for its tanker TCEs applied to every route. The common market is the latest month with dated public bunker prices and an EU allowance price: August 2026."
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      "Port charges have no public per call figure for any study port (the Baltic uses Cory Brothers' estimates, which are not published), so every TCE leaves them out and reads high by their amount.",
      "Canal tolls depend on each ship's own canal tonnage, which is not published for the Baltic's standard ships; a routing through a canal leaves its toll out and says so.",
      "Dry laytime becomes calendar days by the route's terms: Saturdays and Sundays excepted terms count five working days in seven, Sundays and holidays included terms count every day, and the turn time is added. Capesize loading on the port's scale has no published rate and is left out."
